course: dictionary
lesson: 8694
title: The little word self
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word self means you as a person: your own nature and character. «She showed her true self when she helped the lost puppy.» Her true self is the kind person she really is, underneath everything else.
explain: Self can also show that you do something for yourself. In «At a self-checkout you scan your own shopping», the word own is your clue: no helper is at the till, so you do the job yourself.
explain: When self comes at the front of a word with a hyphen, split the word into self and the rest, then ask what the rest means. «Self-conscious» is about feeling that everyone is looking at you. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Self means you as a person: your own nature and character.
fact: The prefix self- means by, of or for yourself, as in «self-service».
fact: «Self-control» means controlling yourself, like the cuttlefish that waited for its favourite shrimp instead of eating a less tasty crab at once.
fact: «Self-conscious» means feeling that everyone notices you, even about a small thing like a spot.
fact: Your usual state is your normal self: «He wasn't his normal self today» means he was not acting the way he usually does.
exercise: «At a self-checkout you scan your own shopping.» What does the self- in self-checkout mean? | Done by a friend next to you | Done by a shop worker for you | Done by the shop's owner at home | *Done by you, for yourself
exercise: Most people don't notice a spot, but feeling that everyone sees it is called being «self-conscious». What does self-conscious mean? | Feeling sleepy after a long day | Being very tall | *Feeling that everyone notices you | Loving your own portrait
exercise: «She showed her true self when she helped the lost puppy.» What is her true self? | A picture of her | *Her own nature and character | Her friend's name | Her shoes
test: The prefix self- means by, of or for yourself. What does «self-defence» mean? | Defending a friend's garden | *Protecting yourself | Defending a shop | Ringing a bell
test: «He wasn't his normal self today.» What does normal self mean here? | His brother's name | His favourite game | *His usual state | His new haircut
exam: The cuttlefish waited for its favourite shrimp instead of eating a crab at once, which needs what? | *Self-control | Self-checkout | Self-portrait | Self-service
